            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "5f21b8525618ef76efa3ebcc25b0071f67ab44669b69027fe6478c4b8a147315"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.2",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "63ae2e03b7ca4dafa9814221b3642dd61eda14f20412d3bc01f45bef6855e36d",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.2,
              "timestamp": "2026-07-26 03:36:34 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/Tool-Calling",
              "cwe": "CWE-185",
              "impact": "An attacker who controls a GitHub repository name containing regex metacharacters could cause unexpected matching behavior or, in worst case, trigger catastrophic backtracking in the regex engine leading to denial of service during tool scanning operations.",
              "technical_analysis": "In scripts/scan-tool.js line 204, the code constructs a regex using interpolated values from the GitHub URL:\n\n```javascript\ndescription = description.replace(new RegExp(`Contribute to ${owner}/${repo}.*?GitHub\\\\.?`, 'i'), '').trim();\n```\n\nThe owner and repo values come from parsing the user-provided URL. While the URL validation regex (`/^https?:\\/\\/github\\.com\\/([^/]+)\\/([^/]+).../`) restricts these to non-slash characters, it does not prevent regex metacharacters like `.`, `*`, `+`, `?`, `(`, `)`, `[`, `]`, `^`, `$`, `{`, `}`, `|`, `\\`. \n\nIf an attacker creates a GitHub repository with a name containing these characters (which GitHub allows), the resulting regex could:\n1. Match unintended strings due to unescaped metacharacters\n2. Cause catastrophic backtracking with crafted input patterns\n3. Alter the description cleaning logic unpredictably\n\nAdditionally, semgrep flagged this as a potential ReDoS vector because the RegExp() constructor is called with a dynamically constructed pattern rather than a hardcoded literal.",
              "remediation_steps": "Escape special regex characters in owner and repo names before interpolating them into the RegExp constructor. Use a helper function like String.prototype.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, '\\\\$&') to escape all regex metacharacters. Alternatively, use string.includes() or string.startsWith() for simple substring matching instead of regex when possible.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Create a GitHub repository with a name containing regex metacharacters, e.g., test.*?.+\n2. Run: node cli.js add https://github.com/attacker/test.*?.+\n3. Observe that the scanner's description cleaning regex matches unintended strings due to the unescaped . and * characters in the repo name.\n4. For ReDoS testing, craft a repository name that triggers catastrophic backtracking when combined with certain description content.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
